commitResults.messageBody:
Implements the stretch goal: an abstract Command class (execute(tasks,
ui, storage), isExit()) with one concrete subclass per action --
ListCommand, MarkCommand, UnmarkCommand, DeleteCommand,
AddTodoCommand, AddDeadlineCommand, AddEventCommand, OnCommand,
ExitCommand, UnknownCommand. Parser.parse(String) now turns a raw
input line directly into the Command to run, reusing the existing
argument-parsing helpers internally.

Chud.main is rewritten to the target's polymorphic loop shape:

    while (!isExit) {
        try {
            String fullCommand = ui.readCommand();
            ui.showLine();
            Command command = Parser.parse(fullCommand);
            command.execute(tasks, ui, storage);
            isExit = command.isExit();
        } catch (ChudException e) {
            ui.showError(e.getMessage());
        } finally {
            ui.showLine();
        }
    }

Chud.java shrank from ~108 lines (with a 9-case switch) to 21.

One deliberate behavior change: "bye" now exits based on its command
word alone, like every other command, rather than an exact-string
match on the whole input line -- so "bye <anything>" now exits too,
where it previously showed "I don't know what 'bye' means". This is
a natural consequence of unifying all commands (including exit) under
the same Parser.parse/Command.execute dispatch; a new test case
covers it. Ui.showGoodbye() was removed in favor of ExitCommand
calling ui.showMessage(...) directly, since the loop's own showLine()
calls now frame every command's output uniformly, including exit's.

Verified via test-ui, 16/16 passing (15 previous + 1 new for the bye
trailing-arguments case).

